WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.760
Fuse a cell that is copying its DNA with one that has not started.

2
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.120
The second nucleus begins copying early.

3
00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:17.160
Fuse it with a cell that has finished, and that nucleus refuses.

4
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.560
Something in the cytoplasm says copy now.

5
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:23.440
What is it?

6
00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:25.953
Frogs gave the first clue.

7
00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:31.673
A frog oocyte waits until the hormone progesterone tells it to go on.

8
00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:37.653
Take cytoplasm from a treated oocyte and inject it into an untreated one, and the

9
00:00:37.653 --> 00:00:38.833
recipient matures.

10
00:00:39.853 --> 00:00:42.693
Removing the donor's nucleus did not stop this.

11
00:00:43.373 --> 00:00:45.653
The activity was named MPF.

12
00:00:46.193 --> 00:00:48.253
It was an activity, not a molecule.

13
00:00:49.167 --> 00:00:54.907
Researchers labelled newly made proteins in sea urchin embryos and followed them

14
00:00:54.907 --> 00:00:56.007
through the first divisions.

15
00:00:57.027 --> 00:01:01.667
One protein built up through each cycle and vanished at each division.

16
00:01:02.427 --> 00:01:03.867
It was named cyclin.

17
00:01:04.767 --> 00:01:07.987
But disappearing at division is only a correlation.

18
00:01:08.807 --> 00:01:12.927
A protein that vanishes could be a result of division, not a cause.

19
00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:18.650
Tests came next, in frog egg extracts that cycle in a tube.

20
00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:24.710
Destroy the extract's own messenger RNA, and the cycles stop in interphase.

21
00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:29.510
Add cyclin messenger RNA back, and the cycles return.

22
00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:34.750
A cyclin that cannot be destroyed holds the extract in mitosis.

23
00:01:35.683 --> 00:01:45.663
The lines met. MPF turned out to be the kinase Cdc2, now called CDK1, bound to

24
00:01:45.663 --> 00:01:51.823
cyclin B. The CDK carries the catalytic site and is inactive alone.

25
00:01:52.603 --> 00:01:54.263
The cyclin switches it on.

26
00:01:54.263 --> 00:02:00.183
Cyclin levels rise and fall, while CDK levels stay fairly even.

27
00:02:01.017 --> 00:02:03.297
Two enzymes tune the switch.

28
00:02:04.277 --> 00:02:07.717
Wee1 puts an inhibitory phosphate on CDK1.

29
00:02:08.657 --> 00:02:17.917
Cdc25 removes it. Active CDK1 also switches on Cdc25 and switches off Wee1, so

30
00:02:17.917 --> 00:02:19.637
mitotic entry is a switch.

31
00:02:20.817 --> 00:02:27.977
In extracts, entry needed about 32 to 40 nanomolar cyclin B. An extract already in

32
00:02:27.977 --> 00:02:31.497
mitosis stayed there down to about 16 to 24.

33
00:02:32.657 --> 00:02:34.497
Making cyclin drives entry.

34
00:02:35.217 --> 00:02:36.737
Destroying it permits exit.
